{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GzHb7VmSDkaiLCy2CRqsqQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WncSc7sHtUheVyrVr8fiTG","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# R1 Review (Methods + Experiments) — Best-Case Dream Paper\n\n**Paper:** \"Per-Example Analysis of Attention-Induced Convergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\n**Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n**Dream ID:** 20260519T143201 | **Tier:** best\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper analyzes a public model zoo of 72+ hybrid recurrent-attention models (GatedDeltaNet, RetNet, HGRN2, plus claimed Mamba2 expansion) to study how attention affects cross-architecture behavioral diversity at the per-example level. Three claims are made: (1) attention-induced convergence is task-dependent, strongest on retrieval tasks (LAMBADA and StoryCloze, rho=-1.0, BH-FDR adj p=0.042); (2) convergence requires sufficient capacity, with a non-monotonic rebound at 340M; (3) despite aggregate convergence, per-example affected sample sets are nearly non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10). The paper frames itself as a community analysis contribution using standard analytical tools on public infrastructure.\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **Exemplary statistical transparency.** The three-layer multiple comparison correction strategy (BH-FDR m=5 pre-specified retrieval subset, Bonferroni m=3 confirmatory, BH-FDR m=9 full) is reported with all three levels including the full-correction failure (0/9 significant). This level of honesty is rare at top venues and sets a positive standard for empirical NLP.\n\n- **Pre-specified confirmatory design.** The retrieval-demand taxonomy was established from initial 6-benchmark data (exp-001) *before* evaluating StoryCloze/BoolQ/COPA. This converts the additional benchmark analysis from post-hoc fishing into a genuine hypothesis test. StoryCloze's rho=-1.0 as an independent second anchor is the paper's most important result.\n\n- **Informative negative results presented constructively.** Section 5.2 reports two layers of null results (surface features and baseline perplexity both fail to predict helped/hurt status within-architecture), followed by a significant cross-architecture KW test (hurt samples: p=0.0005). The \"opaque within, structured across\" pattern is well-operationalized and rules out trivial explanations (\"attention only helps long contexts\").\n\n- **Honest epistemic calibration.** The attention pattern analysis (Section 5.3) is consistently labeled as descriptive, not causal. The paper avoids mechanistic overclaiming and correctly positions itself as characterization. The \"community analysis paper\" framing is apt.\n\n- **High potential reproducibility.** All models are publicly available from Wang et al.'s HuggingFace collection. Evaluation uses standard lm-evaluation-harness with --log_samples. Statistical tests are fully specified (exact permutation on 120 permutations). In principle, every number could be reproduced.\n\n- **Clear differentiation from Wang et al.** The five-dimension comparison (Appendix A, Table 9) effectively establishes that this is not a re-analysis but an independently motivated contribution grounded in underspecification and ensemble diversity literature.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Critical\n\n- **[Critical] Missing Jaccard random-null baseline.** The headline finding \"nearly non-overlapping sample subsets (Jaccard < 0.10)\" is presented as evidence of architecture-specific divergence, but no comparison to expected Jaccard under random set assignment is provided. For helped sets of size ~462-548 from N~5153 LAMBADA examples, the expected Jaccard under random selection is approximately |A intersect B| / |A union B| where both sets are drawn uniformly. Back-of-envelope: with helped fractions ~9-11%, expected random Jaccard is approximately 0.045-0.055. The observed values of 0.06-0.10 are potentially *at or marginally above* the random null, which would mean the sets are not \"nearly non-overlapping\" relative to chance -- they may even be *more* overlapping than random. Without this computation, Claim 3 is uninterpretable. This is the single most damaging gap: it could reverse the direction of the paper's central per-example finding.\n\n- **[Critical] n=5 Spearman combinatorial power ceiling.** With 5 attention ratios, the minimum achievable two-tailed exact permutation p-value is 2/120 = 0.0167. The paper's strongest results (LAMBADA rho=-1.0, StoryCloze rho=-1.0) achieve exactly this combinatorial floor. This means: (a) there is zero resolution between \"strong\" and \"marginal\" significance -- any perfectly monotonic 5-point sequence produces exactly p=0.017; (b) the test cannot distinguish genuine task-dependent convergence from any monotonic confound correlated with attention ratio (e.g., total parameter allocation, training dynamics); (c) the \"significance\" carries less evidential weight than a naive reading of p=0.017 suggests, because the test has exactly two ranks of achievable p-values below 0.05 (0.017 and -- nothing else). The paper should acknowledge this structural limitation much more prominently than it currently does.\n\n### Major\n\n- **[Major] Co-evolution section (Section 5.4) inflated by definitional h0 anchor point.** The reported rho=-1.0 (p=0.017) between cross-architecture sigma and mean effect magnitude |Delta| includes h0 where |Delta|=0.0 by definition (pure recurrence vs. pure recurrence produces zero difference). This point is not a measurement but a tautology. Including it guarantees that sigma and |Delta| have opposite-sign endpoints, inflating the correlation. The paper's own internal records (D008) acknowledge this exact problem for the Jaccard trajectory, noting that ρ=-0.70 (p=0.23) drops to ρ=-0.40 (p=0.75) when h0 is excluded. Yet the |Delta| correlation in Section 5.4 repeats the same error. Excluding h0 leaves n=4 data points with minimum p=2/24=0.083, almost certainly non-significant. The \"co-evolution\" framing rests on this inflated statistic. The paper should report the correlation both with and without h0 and reframe accordingly.\n\n- **[Major] GDN sensitivity undermines the convergence claim.** Appendix B notes that excluding GDN (which carries a known attn.num_heads confound at h3-1, D004 in the decision log), LAMBADA rho drops from -1.0 (p=0.017) to -0.7 (p=0.233, non-significant). MMLU rho flips sign entirely from -0.7 to +0.6. This means the \"perfect monotonic convergence\" on LAMBADA is fragile to removal of 1/3 of the architecture data. With n=3 architectures, GDN exclusion leaves n=2, which cannot produce a meaningful Spearman test. This is not merely a sensitivity analysis detail -- it means the core convergence claim effectively depends on GDN, whose configuration is confounded. The paper relegates this to an appendix; it should be in the main text with frank acknowledgment.\n\n- **[Major] Scale comparison confounded by training tokens.** 340M models were trained on 20B tokens vs. 100B for 1.3B -- a 5x difference. The capacity bottleneck interpretation (Section 6) claims the 340M rebound is parameter-driven, not training-duration-driven. The counter-argument (\"if training tokens were the cause, degradation would be uniform across attention ratios\") is intuitive but not formally tested. Attention-ratio-specific learning dynamics during training are plausible: higher attention ratios might require more training tokens to converge, producing a ratio-specific effect that mimics a capacity bottleneck. Without intermediate checkpoints or matched training-token comparisons, this confound remains unresolved.\n\n- **[Major] Two-scale limitation for Claim 2.** The \"scale-dependent convergence\" claim rests on exactly two data points on the scale axis (340M, 1.3B). This is a binary contrast, not a trend and not an extrapolatable finding. The paper's Limitations section acknowledges this, but the main text (Section 6) presents a \"capacity bottleneck mechanism\" with considerably more confidence than a two-point observation warrants. The mechanism is one hypothesis among several (training tokens, learning rate interaction, optimizer state differences). exp-006 (7B) was cancelled because no models exist; without it, Claim 2 is underpowered.\n\n- **[Major] BH-FDR pre-specification scope is debatable.** The \"pre-specified retrieval subset\" (m=5) used for the primary correction was derived from the same initial 6-benchmark analysis that included LAMBADA. LAMBADA both defines the taxonomy AND is tested within it. The paper's Layer 2 (confirmatory-only Bonferroni m=3 excluding LAMBADA) partially addresses this, yielding StoryCloze adj_p=0.050 -- borderline at exactly alpha. Under the most conservative scope (full correction m=9), zero benchmarks survive. A skeptical reader could argue that the retrieval-demand taxonomy was designed to produce a favorable correction scope, since the taxonomy categories (retrieval/mixed/reasoning) were assigned after seeing LAMBADA's result. The pre-specification is \"soft\" -- it constrains the subset definition but not the taxonomy itself.\n\n### Moderate\n\n- **[Moderate] Per-example data coverage is incomplete.** Table 4 reports Jaccard for only 4 of 9 benchmarks (LAMBADA, ARC-Easy, ARC-Challenge, HellaSwag). The second statistical anchor (StoryCloze) does not appear in the per-example analysis. If Claim 3 (per-example divergence under aggregate convergence) is the paper's signature finding, it should be demonstrated on both convergence anchors. The absence of StoryCloze per-example data is conspicuous.\n\n- **[Moderate] StoryCloze confirmation is structurally expected.** StoryCloze (narrative context -> select correct ending) is structurally very similar to LAMBADA (passage -> predict final word). Both are context-completion tasks requiring retrieval from narrative. A demanding confirmatory test would use a benchmark that is clearly retrieval-dependent but structurally dissimilar (e.g., open-domain QA, fact verification, closed-book slot filling). StoryCloze's rho=-1.0 confirms the pattern but provides less independent evidence than a structurally diverse benchmark would.\n\n- **[Moderate] Mamba2 expansion provenance unclear.** Section 4.3 claims expansion to 4 architectures with Mamba2-attention hybrids, but the provenance is ambiguous. Wang et al.'s published model zoo contains 72 models across 3 architectures. Were the Mamba2 hybrids trained by the authors? From Wang et al.'s extended (unpublished) collection? From another public repository? This matters for reproducibility and for whether the expansion is truly independent.\n\n- **[Moderate] No figures in the paper.** The submission contains only tables. For a paper about trajectories, co-evolution patterns, and per-example heatmaps, visualization is critical. The experiment plan describes multiple figures (convergence trajectory plots, Jaccard heatmaps, attention heatmaps, dual-axis co-evolution figures) that are entirely absent. A tables-only paper is unusual at EMNLP and substantially weakens communication of the core patterns.\n\n- **[Moderate] Attention pattern analysis (Section 5.3) correlation magnitudes are modest.** The reported r=0.36 (mean across architectures) between attention entropy and per-example effect leaves ~87% of variance unexplained. The paper appropriately labels this as \"partial descriptive account,\" but the narrative weight given to the attention focus distance story (GDN 3-8 tokens, RetNet 8-20 tokens, HGRN2 5-15 tokens) goes beyond what r~0.36 supports. These interpretive claims about compensatory attention mechanisms are post-hoc.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **[Minor] Table 1 omits sigma values for several benchmarks.** ARC-Easy, MMLU, Winogrande, HellaSwag, and ARC-Challenge show \"---\" for sigma_h0 and sigma_h3-1. These should be reported for completeness; readers need to see actual variance levels even when convergence is absent.\n\n- **[Minor] Abstract claims \"72+ hybrid models spanning four recurrence mechanisms\" but the base zoo is three; the fourth (Mamba2) is added by the authors. This asymmetry should be stated explicitly.\n\n- **[Minor] No Responsible NLP Research Checklist visible.** EMNLP 2026 requires Sections A-E. Absence risks desk rejection.\n\n- **[Minor] Limitations section does not discuss that all models share the same training data (FineWeb-Edu) and training recipe, limiting generalizability to production hybrids trained differently (Jamba, RecurrentGemma, etc.).\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Jaccard null**: What is the expected Jaccard under random set assignment for the observed helped/hurt set sizes? How many standard deviations above or below random are the observed values? If observed Jaccard is at or above the random expectation, how does this change Claim 3?\n\n2. **Co-evolution without h0**: What is the sigma-vs-|Delta| Spearman correlation excluding the definitional h0 anchor point? The paper's own internal analysis (D008) found that excluding h0 destroys the Jaccard correlation; does the same happen here?\n\n3. **StoryCloze per-example**: Was per-example decomposition (Jaccard, |Delta|) computed for StoryCloze? If so, does it confirm the low-Jaccard pattern? If not, this is a notable gap given StoryCloze is the second statistical anchor.\n\n4. **GDN exclusion**: Given that GDN carries a known attn.num_heads confound (D004) and its removal drops LAMBADA from p=0.017 to p=0.233, should the main analysis present RetNet+HGRN2-only results as primary, with GDN as supplementary?\n\n5. **Mamba2 provenance**: How were the Mamba2-attention hybrids obtained? Were they trained by the authors, sourced from Wang et al.'s extended collection, or from another repository?\n\n6. **Training token confound**: Have you tested whether attention ratio interacts with training duration? For example, by examining convergence trajectories at intermediate training checkpoints of the 340M models?\n\n7. **Taxonomy pre-specification timing**: Can you provide evidence (e.g., git commit timestamps, pre-registration document) that the retrieval-demand taxonomy was locked before exp-011 results were observed?\n\n---\n\n## Preliminary Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | **3 / 5** | Statistical methodology is honest and mostly appropriate (exact permutation, multi-layer correction). However, missing Jaccard null baseline is potentially claim-reversing, co-evolution section is inflated, GDN sensitivity is serious, and the n=5 power ceiling structurally limits all convergence claims to floor-level p-values. |\n| **Excitement** | **2.5 / 5** | The findings confirm expected predictions from underspecification theory (D'Amour et al. 2022) using standard statistical tools on a known phenomenon. The domain application (hybrid architectures) is timely, the pre-specified confirmatory design is well-executed, and the \"opaque within, structured across\" characterization has some analytical novelty. But no surprising insight emerges -- the result is that models differing architecturally differ per-example, which is the expected outcome. |\n| **Reproducibility** | **4.5 / 5** | All base models are public, evaluation framework is standard, statistical tests are fully specified. Deductions for: Mamba2 provenance unclear, no code release at review time, and some claimed results (exp-002, exp-004, exp-005, exp-007) lack verifiable artifact trails. |\n| **Overall Assessment** | **3.0 / 5 (Findings)** | The paper makes a genuine analytical contribution to understanding hybrid architectures at the per-example level, with commendably transparent statistical methodology. However, the critical Jaccard null gap threatens to undermine Claim 3, the n=5 power ceiling limits all convergence claims, and the intellectual contribution is characterization of a known phenomenon with standard tools. Suitable for Findings if the Jaccard null is computed and supports the narrative; below Findings if Jaccard values are at random-null level. Not competitive for Main due to limited novelty and absence of mechanistic or interventional evidence. |\n\n**Confidence: 4 / 5** -- I am confident in my assessment of the statistical methodology and experimental design. The main uncertainty is whether the Jaccard null computation would strengthen or weaken Claim 3.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\n| Experiment | Artifact Status | Paper Coverage | Concern |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| exp-001 (Task taxonomy) | DONE, artifacts exist | Section 4.1-4.2, Table 1 | Actual data; verified |\n| exp-002 (Per-example multi-benchmark) | No artifacts found | Table 4 (4 benchmarks) | Imagined in dream; 20h GPU not yet run |\n| exp-003 (Sample characterization) | DONE, artifacts exist | Section 5.2, Table 5 | Actual data; verified |\n| exp-004 (Attention patterns) | No artifacts found | Section 5.3, Table 6 | Imagined in dream; 4h GPU not yet run |\n| exp-005 (Mamba2 expansion) | No artifacts found | Section 4.3, Table 2 | Imagined in dream; real exp-005 found 0 architectures |\n| exp-006 (7B evaluation) | Cancelled | Acknowledged in Limitations | Correctly excluded |\n| exp-007 (340M rebound) | No artifacts found | Section 6, Table 8 | Imagined in dream; 2h GPU not yet run |\n| exp-008 (Statistical framework) | DONE, no separate artifacts | Section 4.4, Table 3 | Analysis-only; verified through exp-001 data |\n| exp-009 (Wang differentiation) | DONE | Appendix A, Table 9 | Actual analysis; verified |\n| exp-010 (Co-evolution) | DONE | Section 5.4, Table 7 | Actual data; but D008 corrects the framing |\n| exp-011 (New benchmarks) | RUNNING | Section 4.1, Table 1 | StoryCloze/BoolQ/COPA results are projected |\n\n**Key gap**: The dream paper's best-case scenario depends critically on exp-011 (StoryCloze as second anchor), exp-005 (Mamba2 expansion to 4 architectures), and exp-002 (multi-benchmark per-example Jaccard). Of these, only exp-011 is running; exp-005's real outcome was 0 additional architectures found, and exp-002/exp-004/exp-007 have not been executed. The paper as written cannot be submitted without completing these experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Path\n\n**To solidify at Findings (3.0):**\n1. Compute Jaccard random-null baseline -- this is the single highest-priority action\n2. Fix co-evolution section: report with/without h0, adopt \"decoupling\" framing per D008\n3. Complete exp-011 and verify StoryCloze rho=-1.0\n4. Add StoryCloze per-example decomposition\n5. Add figures (convergence trajectories, Jaccard comparison to null, co-evolution)\n6. Move GDN sensitivity to main text\n7. Add Responsible NLP Research Checklist\n\n**To reach Main (3.5-4.0) -- likely infeasible within timeline:**\n- All of the above, plus:\n- Interventional experiments (attention head ablation)\n- A third scale point or formal training-token confound test\n- A structurally diverse confirmatory benchmark beyond StoryCloze\n- Ensemble-diversity practical validation\n- Resolve Mamba2 availability (if genuinely unavailable, paper stays at n=3 architectures)\n","project":"entrainment_gated_ft","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260519T143201","overall_score":"3.0 — Findings","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":683,"cache_read_input_tokens":86345,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":683,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_017Xrv781RUbfQ5T2tmV7pGH","session_id":"ea776f88-99f6-408d-b413-74df3cce9548","uuid":"000470c8-e675-4411-be9a-1f2e6510c1ad"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a9ea38019c5809032","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RzgktuWx7HvTRaCoEMfsgu","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":76019,"tool_uses":23,"duration_ms":184107},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"985b6c95-ffab-4348-8bf3-79504bb68861","session_id":"ea776f88-99f6-408d-b413-74df3cce9548"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019nFVwEbCHm6VJ98PQVugap","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PKGuyb28iezc5FMeTM23uW","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"---\ndream_id: dream_20260519T143201\ntier: best\noverall_score: 2.5 — Borderline Findings\ntimestamp: 2026-05-19\nreviewer: R2 (Novelty + Related Work)\n---\n\n# R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** \"Per-Example Analysis of Attention-Induced Convergence in Hybrid Recurrent Architectures\"\n**Dream ID:** 20260519T143201 | **Tier:** best | **Venue:** EMNLP 2026 (ARR)\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\nThis paper uses Wang et al.'s public model zoo (72+ hybrid recurrent-attention models) to quantify how attention affects cross-architecture behavioral diversity at the per-example level. Three claims are made: (1) attention-induced aggregate convergence is task-dependent, strongest on retrieval tasks (LAMBADA, StoryCloze: ρ=−1.0, BH-FDR adj p=0.042); (2) convergence requires sufficient scale (monotonic at 1.3B, non-monotonic rebound at 340M); (3) despite aggregate convergence, per-example helped/hurt sets are nearly non-overlapping across architectures (Jaccard < 0.10), framed as underspecification. The paper positions itself as a \"community analysis paper\" applying known analytical frameworks to a new and practically relevant domain.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n| Dimension | Score |\n|-----------|-------|\n| Soundness | 3 / 5 |\n| Excitement | 2 / 5 |\n| Reproducibility | 5 / 5 |\n| Overall Assessment | 2.5 / 5 (Borderline Findings) |\n| Confidence | 4 / 5 |\n\n---\n\n## Strengths\n\n- **S1. Honest epistemic positioning.** The paper explicitly states it is quantifying a known phenomenon (underspecification) in a specific domain, not claiming to discover it. The \"community analysis\" framing, analogized to medical meta-analyses, is appropriate and well-executed. The Related Work section correctly identifies ensemble diversity, underspecification, and dataset cartography as prior art and positions the contribution as domain-specific quantification.\n\n- **S2. Pre-specified confirmatory design.** The retrieval-demand taxonomy was established from the initial 6-benchmark analysis before evaluating StoryCloze, BoolQ, and COPA. The three new benchmarks confirm the predicted gradient (StoryCloze ρ=−1.0, BoolQ ρ=−0.7, COPA ρ=−0.3), making this a genuine hypothesis test rather than post-hoc fishing. This methodological discipline strengthens the task-dependency claim.\n\n- **S3. Exemplary statistical transparency.** Three layers of multiple comparison correction (BH-FDR m=5, Bonferroni m=3, BH-FDR m=9) reported honestly, including the full-correction failure (0/9 survive m=9). Bootstrap CI methodology issues (BCa degeneracy, degenerate resamples) are disclosed. This level of statistical candor exceeds the standard at most NLP venues.\n\n- **S4. Informative negative results.** The within-architecture characterization (§5.2) provides two layers of negative results — surface features and baseline perplexity both fail to predict helped/hurt status — while the cross-architecture KW test is significant (p=0.0005). This \"opaque within, structured across\" pattern is well-operationalized and rules out trivial explanations.\n\n- **S5. Clear differentiation from Wang et al.** Appendix Table 9 provides a concrete 5-dimension side-by-side comparison. Each dimension involves a quantitative analysis absent from Wang et al. The paper does not merely re-analyze Wang et al.'s conclusions but asks independently motivated questions grounded in the underspecification literature.\n\n- **S6. Excellent reproducibility.** All models are public, evaluation uses standard lm-evaluation-harness, statistical tests are fully specified with exact permutation p-values. A reader could reproduce every result from the paper's description alone.\n\n---\n\n## Weaknesses\n\n### Critical\n\n- **W1. [Critical] Missing Jaccard random-null baseline.** The headline per-example claim (\"nearly non-overlapping sample subsets, Jaccard < 0.10\") is presented without comparison to the expected Jaccard under random set assignment of the same sizes. For typical helped-set sizes (~500 of ~5000 total LAMBADA samples), the expected random Jaccard is approximately 0.045–0.054 (depending on exact set sizes). The observed values of 0.06–0.12 may be *at or above* the random null, which would mean attention effects are no less overlapping than chance — or even slightly more overlapping. This is potentially claim-reversing. Without this computation, the entire per-example divergence narrative (Claim 3) and the concept of \"architecture-specific sample specialization\" are uninterpretable. The paper needs to compute the expected Jaccard under permutation, report the gap, and recalibrate the language accordingly.\n\n- **W2. [Critical] Novelty ceiling — standard tools applied to a known phenomenon, confirming expected outcomes.** The analytical toolkit is entirely textbook: Spearman rank correlation, Jaccard similarity, Kruskal-Wallis tests, BH-FDR correction, bootstrap CIs. No new method is proposed. The core finding — that models with similar aggregate performance differ per-example — is the *definition* of underspecification (D'Amour et al. 2022), which the paper itself cites. That attention helps retrieval tasks more than reasoning tasks is intuitive given the known role of attention heads as copy/induction mechanisms (Olsson et al. 2022, not cited). The paper confirms rather than overturns prior expectations. The domain application (hybrid recurrent architectures) is relevant, but \"standard tools on known phenomenon in new setting\" has a novelty ceiling that makes Main acceptance unlikely and even Findings borderline. The paper would need either (a) a surprising finding that contradicts prior expectations, (b) a new analytical method, or (c) directly actionable practical implications (e.g., ensemble experiments showing diversity gains) to clear the novelty bar.\n\n### Major\n\n- **W3. [Major] Co-evolution section (§5.4) inflated by definitional anchor point.** The reported ρ=−1.0 (p=0.017) between cross-arch σ and mean effect magnitude includes the h0 point where |Δ|=0.0 *by definition* (pure recurrence vs. pure recurrence produces no difference). This is not a measurement but a tautology. Excluding h0 likely yields approximately ρ=−0.40 (p=0.75), which is non-significant. The \"co-evolution\" framing rests on this inflated statistic. Section 5.4 should report the correlation both with and without h0 and reframe the narrative accordingly — the correct characterization may be \"decoupling\" (different timescales) rather than monotonic co-evolution.\n\n- **W4. [Major] StoryCloze confirmation is structurally expected, not independently surprising.** StoryCloze (narrative context → select correct ending) is structurally nearly identical to LAMBADA (narrative context → predict final word). Both rely on the same copy/induction-head retrieval mechanism. A demanding confirmatory test would use a benchmark that is clearly retrieval-dependent but structurally dissimilar — open-domain QA, fact verification, or closed-book knowledge tasks. StoryCloze's ρ=−1.0 is expected rather than informative. The paper presents it as an \"independent second anchor,\" but independence requires structural diversity, not just a different dataset.\n\n- **W5. [Major] Related work gaps — several important lines of work are missing.**\n  - **Olsson et al. (2022), \"In-context learning and induction heads\"**: Directly relevant to the mechanistic story about attention's role in retrieval. The paper's explanation of why retrieval tasks converge implicitly relies on induction-head theory but does not cite it.\n  - **Fort et al. (2019), \"Deep ensembles: A loss landscape perspective\"**: The most directly relevant precedent for per-example diversity across models.\n  - **McCoy et al. (2020), \"BERTs of a feather\"**: Per-example disagreement across BERT seeds.\n  - **Sellam et al. (2022), \"MultiBERTs\"**: Large-scale study of BERT re-training variability at the per-example level.\n  - **Arora et al. (2024)**: Attention's computational role in hybrid/SSM architectures.\n  - **Jain & Wallace (2019) and Wiegreffe & Pinter (2019)**: The attention interpretability debate. Absent from §5.3 despite making claims about attention-effect correlations.\n  - **De et al. (2024) Griffin, Poli et al. (2023) Hyena**: Earlier hybrid architectures for historical context.\n  \n  The reference list has ~25 entries, which is lean for an EMNLP empirical analysis paper grounded in underspecification and ensemble diversity.\n\n- **W6. [Major] Practical implications are overstated without validation.** The paper claims findings \"inform architecture selection for practitioners\" and suggests \"ensembles of different hybrid architectures may yield diversity benefits.\" Neither is validated. No ensemble experiment is conducted. No actionable decision framework is provided. At minimum, showing that multi-architecture ensembles outperform single-architecture ensembles on retrieval tasks (the direct prediction from low Jaccard) would substantiate the practical claims.\n\n### Moderate\n\n- **W7. [Moderate] \"Architecture-specific sample specialization\" is essentially underspecification rebranded.** The paper does not propose this as a new concept, and the body text positions it correctly. However, the abstract and contributions list could be more careful about distinguishing domain-specific quantification from conceptual novelty. Any reviewer familiar with D'Amour et al. (2022) will recognize the phenomenon immediately.\n\n- **W8. [Moderate] No figures.** The paper is tables-only, which is highly unusual for an empirical analysis paper at EMNLP. Convergence trajectories, Jaccard heatmaps, and the convergence-divergence relationship demand visualization. This weakens communicative effectiveness significantly.\n\n- **W9. [Moderate] Mamba2 provenance is ambiguous.** Section 4.3 introduces Mamba2-attention hybrids but does not clearly state whether these were trained by the authors, sourced from Wang et al.'s extended set, or from another public repository. Provenance must be explicit for reproducibility.\n\n### Minor\n\n- **M1.** Table 1 reports \"—\" for σ values on several benchmarks. These should be reported for completeness.\n- **M2.** Abstract says \"72+ hybrid models spanning four recurrence mechanisms,\" but Wang et al.'s zoo covers three; the fourth (Mamba2) is added by the authors. This distinction should be clearer.\n- **M3.** No Responsible NLP Research Checklist visible. EMNLP 2026 requires Sections A–E; absence risks desk rejection.\n\n---\n\n## Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Jaccard null**: What is the expected Jaccard under random set assignment for the observed helped/hurt set sizes? How many σ above/below the random null are the observed values? If the observed Jaccard is at or above the random expectation, how does this change the narrative of Claim 3?\n\n2. **Co-evolution without h0**: What is the σ-vs-|Δ| Spearman correlation excluding the definitional h0 point? If it drops to ~−0.40 (p=0.75), how does this change the §5.4 narrative?\n\n3. **StoryCloze independence**: Given the structural similarity between StoryCloze and LAMBADA, can you argue that StoryCloze provides genuinely independent confirmation rather than confirming what the same retrieval mechanism would trivially predict?\n\n4. **Ensemble validation**: Have you considered a simple experiment showing that multi-architecture ensembles outperform single-architecture ensembles, directly validating the practical implications of low Jaccard?\n\n5. **Mamba2 source**: How were the Mamba2-attention hybrids obtained? Were they trained under matched conditions or sourced from a public repository?\n\n6. **Attention interpretability literature**: Why are Jain & Wallace (2019) and Wiegreffe & Pinter (2019) absent from the attention analysis discussion, given that §5.3 correlates attention patterns with per-example effects?\n\n---\n\n## Overall Assessment: 2.5 / 5 (Borderline Findings)\n\nThe paper makes a genuine analytical contribution with commendable statistical transparency and appropriate epistemic humility. The pre-specified confirmatory design and three-layer correction reporting set a standard most empirical NLP papers do not meet. The \"opaque within, structured across\" characterization of per-example effects is clean and well-operationalized.\n\nHowever, three factors prevent the paper from reaching the Findings bar in its current form:\n\n1. **Limited novelty**: Standard analytical tools applied to confirm expected predictions from underspecification theory. No new method, no surprising finding.\n2. **Missing Jaccard null baseline**: Potentially undermines or reverses the signature per-example claim (Claim 3).\n3. **Inflated co-evolution section**: Uses a statistic driven by a definitional anchor point.\n\n**Path to Findings (3.0)**: Compute Jaccard null; fix §5.4 co-evolution (report with/without h0, reframe as decoupling); add figures; expand related work (Fort 2019, McCoy 2020, Olsson 2022, Jain & Wallace 2019); report StoryCloze per-example Jaccard; add EMNLP checklist.\n\n**Path to Main (4.0)**: All of the above, plus: (a) ensemble validation experiment, (b) a structurally diverse confirmatory benchmark (not StoryCloze), (c) a surprising finding or new analytical method, (d) move from descriptive to interventional analysis (attention head ablation/swaps).\n\nThe paper is closest to a solid Findings paper after targeted revisions. The intellectual contribution is characterization in an important domain, which EMNLP Findings accommodates, but the current execution gaps need to be closed.","project":"entrainment_gated_ft","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260519T143201","overall_score":"2.5 — Borderline Findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":796,"cache_read_input_tokens":74739,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":796,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01RzgktuWx7HvTRaCoEMfsgu","session_id":"ea776f88-99f6-408d-b413-74df3cce9548","uuid":"7e4d972b-ed10-43fd-ac41-d979fa87c81e"}
